In France, voting traditionally takes place without modern technologies: the electorate isolate in the booth and put a pre-printed sheet of paper in the envelope, stating the candidate they have elected.
After officials verifying the identity of the elector, the electorator throws the envelope into the urn and signs a list of the electors.
The French Electoral Act contains quite strict codification of the election.
From 1988, the ballot boxes must be transparent so that the electors and observers can testify that there were no envelopes in them even before the start of the vote, and that there have been no envelopes other than those deposited and authorized by the electorate.
Candidates can evict representatives who then witness the whole process. In the evening, the votes are counted by volunteers under strict supervision and according to specific procedures.
